{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794485306,"nonce":"e1uHknFoDldTEg2JjeQ+nbZAxZk=","message":"Committed as bb07c18, with 722c3e3 and eca5429 for what binding sqlite3 found in hxn itself. Asked for on 2026-11-12: callbacks, out-parameters, borrowed objects, and binding a real library.\n\nReal library: sqlite3 from the macOS SDK's header, imported with `hxn import $SDK/usr/include/sqlite3.h --link sqlite3`, no edits. 169 functions imported, 150 listed as not imported. A program that opens `:memory:`, creates a table, inserts, queries through `prepare_v2`/`step`/`column_*` and reads `errmsg` runs on Ash and hxcpp (tests/cimport/sqlite). What it found, in order:\n- Rust keywords as C names (`try`), and a void wrapper that could fail returning nothing: fixed in the generator.\n- camelCase parameter names: names are now snake case, the C name kept in `HxnC`.\n- `sqlite3_close` returns an int, so the destructor was not recognised and became a method `close`, which every hxn class has: a destructor may return int (`[HxnFreeInt]`, so the declaration keeps the real signature, which wasm checks), and a method named `close`, `same` or `weak` is renamed with `_c`.\n- The hxcpp link failed: a Rust static library does not carry `-lsqlite3` to the program. Fixed in hxn for every library (eca5429): see the CLI issue.\n- The CPPIA host, which links hxcpp's bundled sqlite, reported duplicate symbols: hxn exported `sqlite3_value_subtype` for the interface `Value` of library `sqlite3`, the name of sqlite3's own function. In the builds that linked, the wrapper called its own export. Fixed in hxn for every library (722c3e3): see the generator issue.\n- sqlite3's comments start lines with `**`: stripped.\n\nOut-parameters (`[HxnOut]`, `[HxnNull]`): a non-const pointer to a number, enum, struct, text or object pointer after every passed parameter. The operation returns it; an int C return becomes `[HxnStatus]`; later outputs are passed null. An object written through a pointer is owned before the status check, so it is freed on failure (sqlite3_open allocates on failure). A function writing an object through a pointer is that object's constructor or static (`sqlite3_open` -\u003e `new Sqlite3(path)`).\n\nBorrowed objects (`[HxnBorrowed]`): every wrapper has `owned`; a borrowed one is not freed. The importer guesses borrowed for an object a method of another object returns (`sqlite3_db_handle`). The wrapper does not keep the owner alive: a borrowed object used after its owner is closed is a dangling pointer, as in C.\n\nThread-safe objects (`[HxnThreadSafe]`): methods take `\u0026self` and the type is `Sync`. Never guessed.\n\nCallbacks: a function pointer followed by `void *`, as a WebIDL `callback` (`[HxnUser=first|last]`, `[HxnStop=n]`). Rust: an hxn lent function `impl FnMut(..) -\u003e Result\u003cT, E\u003e` with `E: From\u003cString\u003e`; a generated `extern \"C\"` trampoline per callback type, generic in the closure, reached through a helper function because the closure's type has no name. The trampoline catches a panic and keeps it with a failure, returns `HxnStop` to C, and is not called again after a failure; once C returns, the panic is resumed or the Haxe function's error returned ahead of the call's own. The body runs inside an immediately called closure so its String errors convert to E at one place.\n- Weighed: storing the closure in a `Box` and passing a `dyn` pointer. The generic trampoline needs no allocation and no vtable, and the type checks the bound.\n- sqlite3's callback functions mostly keep the callback (`busy_handler`, `commit_hook`, `trace`, `progress_handler`, `set_authorizer`), which a lent function cannot outlive. The importer refuses a function whose name has one of `set register install hook handler listen listener subscribe watch on trace profile progress busy notify`, and lists every other callback function for review. This is a name heuristic and will be wrong for some libraries.\n- `sqlite3_exec`'s callback takes `char **` arrays, so it is not imported.\n\nAlso: on Windows, HashLink printed non-ASCII text as mojibake (the console code page), so tests/cimport compares non-ASCII text inside Haxe and prints only ASCII. The binding itself carried the text correctly.\n\nRun on 2026-11-12, macOS: tests/cimport/run.sh on Ash (hybrid, JIT, interpreter), hxcpp, CPPIA and Emscripten (sqlite3 skipped under Emscripten, which has no sqlite3.h); with it the whole suite after the symbol rename: generated fixture on Ash (three modes), hxcpp, CPPIA, wasm, Emscripten; examples and CLI on Ash, hxcpp, CPPIA, wasm, Emscripten; iOS simulator; stock HashLink on the NUC (fixture and examples). Not run: Windows and Linux for these three commits (CI), Android since bb07c18.\n\nStill open: callbacks that C keeps (needs a registration model where Haxe owns the function and the library is told when it is released), out-parameter arrays, C++, `hl --wasm` for C code.","files":null}]}